            make_q("What happens in DBSCAN if $\\epsilon$ (eps) is chosen too small?", ["All points merge into 1 cluster", "Most data points fail to meet the density threshold and are classified as noise (-1)", "The computer memory overflows", "The algorithm converts into K-Means"], 1, "An excessively tiny $\\epsilon$ means neighborhoods rarely contain enough points, falsely flagging genuine clusters as noise."),
            make_q("What happens in DBSCAN if $\\epsilon$ (eps) is chosen too large?", ["All points are classified as noise", "Most or all clusters merge into a single giant connected cluster", "The algorithm fails to run", "The number of clusters approaches infinity"], 1, "An overly large $\\epsilon$ bridges distinct density valleys, erroneously consolidating separate groups into one giant cluster."),
            make_q("How can the k-distance graph (elbow of nearest neighbor distances) be used to select $\\epsilon$ in DBSCAN?", ["By plotting the training accuracy", "By plotting the sorted distance of every point to its $k$-th nearest neighbor; the 'elbow' inflection point indicates optimal $\\epsilon$", "By measuring GPU clock frequency", "By setting $\\epsilon = k$"], 1, "A sorted k-NN distance plot shows a sharp threshold (elbow) where noise points diverge from dense cluster points, indicating ideal $\\epsilon$."),
            make_q("Why does standard DBSCAN struggle on datasets containing clusters with significantly differing densities?", ["DBSCAN only works on binary data", "A single global $\\epsilon$ cannot simultaneously capture dense clusters (requiring small $\\epsilon$) and sparse clusters (requiring large $\\epsilon$)", "DBSCAN requires all clusters to have equal sample counts", "DBSCAN converts density to variance"], 1, "Because $\\epsilon$ is fixed globally, a setting that isolates sparse clusters will blur dense clusters together, and vice versa."),
