            make_q("What is Kernel PCA (KPCA)?", ["A kernel written in C++", "A non-linear extension of PCA that uses the kernel trick (RBF, Polynomial) to perform PCA in a high-dimensional feature space, unfolding non-linear manifolds", "A method for compressing kernels", "A regularizer for decision trees"], 1, "Kernel PCA applies the kernel trick to map data into Hilbert space before PCA, enabling non-linear dimensionality reduction (e.g. concentric circles)."),
            make_q("What is Incremental PCA (`IncrementalPCA` in Scikit-learn)?", ["PCA that only increases eigenvalues", "A variant of PCA that processes data in mini-batches, allowing PCA on massive datasets that cannot fit entirely into computer RAM", "PCA that runs on GPUs", "A method for adding columns"], 1, "IncrementalPCA updates SVD in chunks, enabling out-of-core dimensionality reduction on datasets larger than physical memory."),
            make_q("What does `n_components=0.95` signify in Scikit-learn's `PCA(n_components=0.95)`?", ["It retains exactly 95 features", "It automatically selects the minimum number of principal components required to preserve at least 95% of total cumulative variance", "It sets learning rate to 0.95", "It drops 95% of the data"], 1, "Passing a float between 0 and 1 tells Scikit-learn to retain enough components to meet the specified cumulative variance threshold."),
            make_q("Are the principal components generated by PCA interpretable in terms of original physical features?", ["Yes, each component corresponds to exactly 1 original feature", "No, each principal component is a linear combination (weighted mixture) of all original features, making direct individual interpretation difficult", "Yes, components retain original column names", "They have no mathematical meaning"], 1, "Principal components are linear combinations $\\sum v_{ij} x_j$; individual components blend many features, obscuring individual domain semantics."),
